[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1976","stub":{"id":"1976","section":"Below Staunton Dam to Spring Creek (Route 727)","altname":"","river":"North","difficulty":"IItoIV","states":["USA-VRG"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"108995","location":{"latitude":"38.3330993652344","longitude":"-79.2060012817383"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"114230","location":{"latitude":"38.3946990966797","longitude":"-79.0309982299805"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"26","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101247484","title":null,"post_date":"2010-12-01 05:00:00+00","detail":"Paddled it today at levels between 6-700cfs on the Stokesville gauge. With the side streams coming in, I'd say it was over 1000cfs of actual flow by the take-out. The rapids were largely class 3-3+, with one section that was low Class 4 (towards where the river leaves the forest, consists of a series of slides and big wave trains). The minimum is definitely too low, you could easily run this over 1000cfs, just have to be on the lookout for strainers.\n\nThere is one that's been there for a few years; it comes after the river splits around an island about 1.5 miles in. There is a shallow channel that juts off 90 degrees to the left, at low flows the right channel was a beaver dam, at high flow, it looked like the correct channel to take. Problem is, when these channels come back together, a really nasty strainer is across the entire river, chest high, with branches sticking out from it that make it very dangerous because there are no eddies after the channels come together. In the winter, it collects ice balls making it a river-wide wall. The key is to take the shallow channel and get out before the two merge back together.","user":{"name":"CHRIS PREPERATO"}}],"photos":[{"id":"7710","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7551","description":"This is below the second of the two large drops.  Left side of the picture shows a decent sneak boof into a calm pool, while the far right shows the slide into a diagonal hole that was somewhat sticky.  Note the rooster tails off the piton rocks jutting out slightly from the rock face.  Angle your boat right!","caption":"Main drop near the takeout","poi_name":"Largest and last rapid","subject":"","author":"Chris B.","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7710","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7710"}},{"id":"10631","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7054","description":"Jeremy surfs one of the many superior ledges in the North River Gorge, the crown jewel of the Shenandoah feeder streams.","caption":"The worm in the Gorge","poi_name":"","subject":"Jeremy Wimpey","author":"C. FInley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10631","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10631"}},{"id":"10633","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7055","description":"Talyor (AKA Bad Ass OC'er) making it look easy.  This is one of the many sweet surf ledges in the Gorge, the crown jewel of the Shenandoah feeder streams.","caption":"Taylor showcases his skills","poi_name":"endless ledges","subject":"Taylor","author":"C. Finley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10633","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10633"}},{"id":"10632","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7055","description":"Joel in Whitewater shock after a rude run-in with a strainer.","caption":"Joel M. displays his battle wounds","poi_name":"","subject":"Joel Maynard","author":"C. FInley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10632","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10632"}},{"id":"881830","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"884471","description":"Best takeout is between the 1st and 2nd bridge at Camp May Flather.  Don't want to go under this one at high water.  Big riverwide strainer pileup just after it too.","caption":"2nd bridge at Camp May Flather","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Justin Abel","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/881830","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/881830"}},{"id":"881829","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"884470","description":"~500 cfs by the Stokesville gauge.  Right at 2ft on the paddlers' gauge at bridge at North River Campground.  ","caption":"Main drop at high water","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Justin Abel","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/881829","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/881829"}},{"id":"8643","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7324","description":"","caption":"Stokesville HEC-RAS model","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Bill Kirby","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8643","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8643"}},{"id":"8621","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7325","description":"This is the North River at the Stokesville gage site.  This is several miles above the reservoir.  During the Frances flooding in September 04 3000cfs went through here!","caption":"Stokesville Gauge","poi_name":"","subject":"North River Gauge Site","author":"Bill Kirby","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8621","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8621"}}],"detail":{"id":"1976","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This is a whitewater run through a remote beautiful gorge. Challenges include ledges, sloping ledges and Alder thickets at high water. This river has the unfortunate distinction of being one of the few VA rivers where a paddling fatality has occurred. There is a hiking trail that crosses the river frequently for scouting and rescue. It's a beautiful hike as well.\n\nSee Also:\n\n_Classic Virginia Rivers,_ Ed Grove (1992 ed), p. 150.\n\n_Virginia Whitewater_, Roger Corbett (2000 ed.), p. 113.\n\n_Exploring Virginia's Waterways_, Ed Gertler (2022 ed.), p. 43.","averageGradient":"15","maxGradient":"46","length":"16.9","correlations":[{"reachID":"1976","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"100","beginMediumRunnable":"200","beginHighRunnable":"300","endHighRunnable":"330"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"NORTH RIVER NEAR STOKESVILLE, VA","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"01620500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"1","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"2.07","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"38.335","longitude":"-79.2391667"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/01620500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/01620500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"me